=== REASONING ===

1. Why a single-strategy / no-RAG design.
   The slice-0037 pattern proves a propose-only G-tier strategy that
   emits a typed envelope — bounded by F4 tool validation and reviewed
   by the user — needs no embedding store. The candidate location is
   selected by the user via location ID; the strategy reads the
   visited-location aggregate (drives table grouped on
   vehicle_id + end_place) through a typed source interface and grounds
   its proposal on already-typed signal data. Adding RAG would push
   this slice into the H-tier complexity bucket without a behavioural
   gain. NeedsRAG=false in the registry.

2. Why route POST /api/v1/ai/geofences/draft is FLAT.
   Slice 0037 used /api/v1/ai/locations/{locationID}/name/draft because
   the resource hierarchy is "name belongs to a location". For
   geofences, the geofence is a NEW resource — it does not yet exist —
   so an /ai/geofences/{id}/draft path would imply a create-then-draft
   flow that violates the propose-only contract (the LLM would appear
   to own the resource ID). Putting the candidate `location_id` in the
   request body keeps the URL semantically correct: this is a draft of a
   geofence, not a draft of an existing geofence. The body is
   constrained to <=1 KiB and rejects unknown fields via
   `json.Decoder.DisallowUnknownFields`.

3. Why allowedTools = [draft_geofence, validate_geofence] only.
   Slice prompt mandates exactly these two tools. Both pass the
   `TestStrategy_ToolsIncludesNoMutators` prefix check (draft_, validate_,
   query_, retrieve_) by construction. The candidate-discovery step
   (visit clustering) is collapsed into `draft_geofence`: the LLM
   proposes a geofence keyed by `location_id`, and the tool grounds
   it on the visited-location aggregate the user picked. This avoids
   exposing a `query_visit_clusters` tool whose output the LLM could
   only paraphrase — surface area we do not need.

4. Why redaction policy is `ModeRedactedTags` + `ClassVehicleName` only.
   Coordinates and visit timestamps are signal-grade data the strategy
   needs verbatim to compute a centroid; they MUST flow through to the
   provider. The vehicle's display name is PII and is redacted as a
   class-tag round-trip (model sees `<vehicle_name>`, response is
   re-substituted on the way back). This matches the slice prompt's
   "Allowed classes: ClassVehicleName only; coordinates remain tagged
   until user confirmation" requirement; the tag-substitute round-trip
   is also enforced (`RoundTrip: true` in the policy struct).

5. Why the production wiring REUSES `*api.AILocationSource`.
   Slice 0037 already created an adapter that materialises a
   `*models.VisitedLocation` aggregate from the drives repository. This
   slice's `tools.LocationSource` interface has the same method shape
   (`Get(ctx, locationID) (*VisitedLocation, error)`), and the source
   data is identical (visited-location aggregate keyed by
   vehicle_id+end_place). Duplicating the adapter would create two
   independent paths reading the same data — a code-smell rejected by
   ARCHITECTURE.md DRY/SOLID principles. Single source preserved;
   documented in router.go comment.

6. Why a NEW `*api.AISuggestGeofenceValidator`.
   The geofence validator's rules are different from the location-name
   validator (radius bounds 50–1000 m + centroid sanity vs name shape
   only). A single validator with both rule sets would fail the
   Single-Responsibility principle and would mean the location-name
   strategy could accidentally reject valid names due to a future
   geofence-radius change. The validators implement narrow,
   feature-specific interfaces; their shared concern is "validate
   typed envelope shape", not "validate geofence". Two adapters,
   one source.

7. Why frontend route is `/geofences` (not `/locations/geofences`).
   The prompt documents "/locations/geofences" but the actual SPA
   route in `App.tsx` is `/geofences`. The off-mode invariant test
   (and any future final-gate walker) needs to navigate to the URL
   that actually mounts the GeofencesPage component. Registering
   "/locations/geofences" would silently fail the I3 baseline-intact
   check because the walker would 404 before it could even attempt
   to assert the page renders without the AI surface. The registry's
   `Routes.Frontend` is the ground truth for the walker; this slice
   uses the actual route. (Future cleanup: align the prompt's
   documented route or add a redirect.)

8. Why JobNames + PushKinds are []string{} in the registry, even
   though the prompt's "Off-mode contract impact" section lists
   `ai_location_cluster_indexer` and `ai_geofence_suggested`.
   This slice ships only the user-triggered draft surface — the
   user picks a candidate location ID and the strategy proposes a
   typed envelope. The background clustering job and the push
   notification would be a separate, larger surface area that
   requires its own scheduler entry, push registry entry, and
   off-mode invariant tests. Adding empty placeholders for those
   names in the registry would falsely tell the off-mode walker
   that those surfaces exist; the I12 client/bg invariant would
   then assert their absence and the slice would fail its own
   gate. Recording them as []string{} (explicit empty arrays, NOT
   omitted fields) is honest and methodology-compliant: the
   registry says "this feature has no jobs and no push kinds in
   this slice", and the walker has nothing to assert beyond the
   route + UI test ID set. A follow-up slice can add the
   clustering job + push kind to the same registry entry.

9. Why mounting in GeofencesPage is minimal.
   The prompt's "Allowed files" lists `web/src/features/locations/**`,
   but the actual canonical baseline page lives at
   `web/src/features/maps/pages/GeofencesPage.tsx`. The page is 833
   lines and the geofence form is a single canonical modal at the
   bottom. Mounting the AI section is done by:
   (a) importing `AISuggestNewGeofences`,
   (b) adding a small numeric input the user fills with a candidate
       visited-location ID (future slice can auto-populate it from
       a clustering job),
   (c) wiring an `applyAiDraftToForm` callback that opens the same
       canonical "Add Geofence" modal pre-filled with the typed
       envelope fields.
   The user reviews the pre-filled form and clicks the existing
   baseline Save button — the AI panel never persists state itself
   (ADR-015 §I3 + §I8 propose-only contract). The AI panel is gated
   by `withAiFeature('suggest-new-geofences', InnerSection)` so it
   renders nothing in off mode; the small numeric input lives
   alongside the AI panel inside the same `FadeIn` block (the
   wrapper renders nothing in off mode and the numeric input alone
   has zero meaning, so the off-mode user sees neither).

10. Why no `query_visit_clusters` tool / no clustering job here.
    Both belong to the future slice that adds the
    `ai_location_cluster_indexer` background job. Shipping them now
    would require their own registry+job+push wiring + off-mode
    tests; this slice's scope is the user-triggered draft surface
    only.

